<h1>Controllers and Action Methods in ASP.NET Core MVC</h1>

<p>
    A Controller is a component in MVC that handles incoming HTTP requests,
    processes application logic, and returns a response.
</p>

<p>
    It acts as a bridge between the Model and the View.
</p>

<hr />

<h2>What is a Controller?</h2>

<p>
    A Controller is responsible for:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>Handling HTTP requests</li>
    <li>Processing business logic</li>
    <li>Returning responses</li>
</ul>

<p>
    Responses can be:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>View</li>
    <li>JSON</li>
    <li>Plain Text</li>
    <li>Redirect</li>
</ul>

<hr />

<h2>Controller Basics</h2>

<p>
    A controller is simply a class that:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>Inherits from <strong>Controller</strong></li>
    <li>Ends with the word <strong>Controller</strong></li>
</ul>

<h3>Example</h3>

<pre>
<code>
public class StudentController : Controller
{

}
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    By default, the route becomes:
</p>

<pre>
<code>
/Student/Index
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h2>What is an Action Method?</h2>

<p>
    An Action Method is a public method inside a controller
    that handles a specific request.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<pre>
<code>
public IActionResult Index()
{
    return View();
}
</code>
</pre>

<h3>Rules for Action Methods</h3>

<ul>
    <li>Must be public</li>
    <li>Cannot be static</li>
    <li>Should return IActionResult (best practice)</li>
</ul>

<hr />

<h2>Types of Action Results</h2>

<hr />

<h3>1. ViewResult</h3>

<pre>
<code>
return View();
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    Returns an HTML page using Razor View.
</p>

<hr />

<h3>2. ContentResult</h3>

<pre>
<code>
return Content("Hello");
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    Returns plain text response.
</p>

<hr />

<h3>3. JsonResult</h3>

<pre>
<code>
return Json(new { name = "Kunal" });
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    Returns JSON data.
</p>

<hr />

<h3>4. RedirectResult</h3>

<pre>
<code>
return Redirect("https://google.com");
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    Redirects the request to another URL.
</p>

<hr />

<h3>5. RedirectToAction</h3>

<pre>
<code>
return RedirectToAction("Index");
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    Redirects to another action method.
</p>

<p>
    We can also redirect to an action method of another controller.
</p>

<pre>
<code>
return RedirectToAction("Index", "Products");
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h2>IActionResult (Core Concept)</h2>

<p>
    A controller action can return different types of responses.
</p>

<p>
    <strong>IActionResult</strong> is a common return type
    that allows returning different action results from the same action method.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<pre>
<code>
public IActionResult Index()
{
    return View();
}
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h2>HTTP Methods in MVC</h2>

<hr />

<h3>GET Request</h3>

<p>
    GET requests are used to fetch data.
</p>

<pre>
<code>
[HttpGet]
public IActionResult Index()
{
    return View();
}
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h3>POST Request</h3>

<p>
    POST requests are used to send data to the server.
</p>

<pre>
<code>
[HttpPost]
public IActionResult Create(Student s)
{
    return View();
}
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h2>Multiple Actions in One Controller</h2>

<p>
    A single controller can contain multiple action methods.
</p>

<pre>
<code>
public class StudentController : Controller
{
    public IActionResult Index()
    {
        return View();
    }

    public IActionResult Details()
    {
        return View();
    }
}
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h2>How Controller Request Flow Works</h2>

<pre>
<code>
User Request
      ↓
Routing System
      ↓
Controller
      ↓
Action Method
      ↓
Response Returned
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h2>Important Interview Point</h2>

<p>
    A Controller in ASP.NET Core MVC handles incoming HTTP requests,
    executes business logic, and returns different types of responses
    using action methods.
</p>